WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.418
On a real Windows PC you would open Windows Security and Event Viewer.

2
00:00:05.418 --> 00:00:12.158
In the CTM Virtual Lab we read the same information with PowerShell, the way many analysts do.

3
00:00:12.158 --> 00:00:19.113
In this demonstration I check Microsoft Defender's status, its protection history, its event log,

4
00:00:19.113 --> 00:00:23.271
my own logon, and the Wazuh agent on the demo workstation.

5
00:00:24.371 --> 00:00:26.805
First, is the antivirus healthy?

6
00:00:26.805 --> 00:00:31.748
Get-MpComputerStatus, piped to Format-List with three properties.

7
00:00:31.748 --> 00:00:38.364
Real-time protection is True, and the signature version and last update time are shown.

8
00:00:38.364 --> 00:00:43.307
If real-time protection were False, that alone would be an alert.

9
00:00:44.407 --> 00:00:46.196
Protection history.

10
00:00:46.196 --> 00:00:50.243
Get-MpThreat lists what Defender has found.

11
00:00:50.243 --> 00:00:55.137
One threat: the EICAR test file, with severity ID 5.

12
00:00:55.137 --> 00:00:57.584
It is not active any more.

13
00:00:58.683 --> 00:01:05.595
Get-MpThreatDetection adds the details an analyst needs: which user, when it was first detected,

14
00:01:05.595 --> 00:01:11.859
which process wrote the file, and the exact path, in the Downloads folder of trainee07.

15
00:01:12.959 --> 00:01:17.281
The same detection is written to Defender's event log.

16
00:01:17.281 --> 00:01:25.364
Get-WinEvent with a filter hashtable picks the Defender Operational log and event IDs 1116, detected,

17
00:01:25.364 --> 00:01:27.205
and 1117, action taken.

18
00:01:27.205 --> 00:01:34.967
Format-List shows the full message: the threat name, severity, path, and the action, quarantined.

19
00:01:36.067 --> 00:01:37.953
Now the Security log.

20
00:01:37.953 --> 00:01:42.802
I filter event ID 4624 and take only the newest event.

21
00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:50.256
Logon type 2 means someone logged on at the keyboard, and the account is trainee07.

22
00:01:50.256 --> 00:01:54.835
In Activity 4.4 you record your own logon this way.

23
00:01:55.935 --> 00:01:59.786
Last: is the SIEM still receiving logs from this PC?

24
00:01:59.786 --> 00:02:03.933
Get-Service Wazuh star shows the Wazuh agent is Running.

25
00:02:03.933 --> 00:02:08.079
A stopped agent means the SOC is blind on that computer.

26
00:02:09.179 --> 00:02:14.167
On a real Windows computer the same information is in these places.

27
00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:19.452
Protection history is in Windows Security, Virus and threat protection.

28
00:02:19.452 --> 00:02:22.057
The event logs are in Event Viewer.

29
00:02:22.057 --> 00:02:28.235
If your trainer provides the Wazuh dashboard, you can check agent status there too.

30
00:02:29.335 --> 00:02:30.154
Your turn.

31
00:02:30.154 --> 00:02:34.573
In the CTM Virtual Lab, choose Day 4 and Activity 4.4.

32
00:02:34.573 --> 00:02:42.266
On your own WKS-LAB workstation, run these commands and record what you find: Defender status,

33
00:02:42.266 --> 00:02:51.350
the threat in your protection history, the time of event 1116, your own 4624 logon, and the Wazuh agent status.

34
00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:55.687
Your workstation has a different detection from mine.

